# Untitled

## Coordination-Induced Interlinked Covalent- and Metal–Organic-Framework Hybrids for Enhanced Lithium Storage
Covalent organic frameworks (COF) or metal–organic frameworks have attracted significant attention for various applications due to their intriguing tunable micro/mesopores and composition/functionality control. Herein, a coordination-induced interlinked hybrid of imine-based covalent organic frameworks and Mn-based metal–organic frameworks (COF/Mn-MOF) based on the \(\mathbf{Mn-N}\) bond is reported. The effective molecular-level coordination-induced compositing of COF and MOF endows the hybrid with unique flower-like microsphere morphology and superior lithium-storage performances that originate from activated Mn centers and the aromatic benzene ring. In addition, hollow or core–shell MnS trapped in N and S codoped carbon (MnS@NS-C-g and MnS@NS-C-l) are also derived from the COF/ Mn-MOF hybrid and they exhibit good lithium-storage properties. The design strategy of COF–MOF hybrid can shed light on the promising hybridization on porous organic framework composites with molecular-level structural adjustment, nano/microsized morphology design, and property optimization.
With the aim of controlling materials with advanced functions via the molecular-level design, exploration on the porous organic materials has flourished in recent years.[1] Covalent organic frameworks (COF) and metal–organic frameworks (MOF), typical representatives of organic and inorganic–organic framework materials, have developed rapidly as new star materials. COF, porous skeleton with a periodic organic building blocks consisting of light elements (C, N, B, etc.) via covalent bonds, has been endowed with lower weight density and molecular-level design characteristic via different functional groups decoration.[1a–e] On the other hand, MOF is constructed by metal ions or metal-containing clusters (secondary building
units, SBUs) and coordinated organic ligands via the more flexible coordination bonds, which results in the controllable morphology and pore characteristic.[1f,g] Induced by these structural merits, COF and MOF have been widely applied in gas storage/separation, catalysis, and photoelectric conversion, and even the field of energy storage including Li-ion batteries, supercapacitors, and hydrogen storage.[2]
In order to effectively combine the merits of COF and MOF and acquire the maximized performances, there are few recent reports regarding the hybridization of COF and MOF.[3] By introducing the as-prepared MOF into the synthetic process of COF, MOF@COF core–shell[3a,b] or MOF-coated COF[3c] hybrids were obtained. All these hybrids have been demonstrated with excellent photocatalytic performances as the effective photo
catalysts/photocatalysis platforms for degradation of rhodamine B,[3a] dehydrogenation of ammonia borane,[3b] and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution,[3c] respectively. This kind of approach (introducing the as-prepared MOF into the synthetic process of COF) would result in the comparatively simple combination of COF and MOF with core–shell or coating composite mode (no principal morphology change), and the molecular-level interlinked hybridization between COF and MOF remains unexplored.
For the purpose of property optimization, intimate hybridization between two components is highly desirable, which may lead to further morphology adjustment, and consequent performance improvement. Considering the fact that organic groups from COF may also coordinate with metal ions of MOF, we design a COF/Mn-MOF hybrid structure with flower-like morphology, which is different from pristine COF or Mn-MOF. A strong synergistic effect relative to new active sites from MOF and COF for lithium storage is observed in the composite. Hollow or core–shell microspheres of \(\mathrm{MnS@N/S}\) codoped carbon can also be derived with superior electrochemical properties.
The COF/Mn-MOF composite with benchmarked pristine COF or Mn-MOF (Figure S1a,b, Supporting Information) was characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD), Fourier transform infrared (FTIR), Raman and \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) nuclear magnetism measurements in Figure S2a–d (Supporting Information). All characteristic diffraction peaks for \(\mathrm{Mn-MOF^{[4]}}\) can be detected for COF/ Mn-MOF with clear shift to small angle for its two main peaks \((2\theta\approx10.5^{\circ}\) and \({\approx}21.7^{\circ}\) ), which is probably originated from the
interaction between COF and Mn-MOF structure (Figure S2a, Supporting Information). It is noteworthy that the introduction of Mn-MOF into the pore structure of COF based on the coordination interaction would disturb the periodicity of the porous structure of COF and similar phenomenon has also been witnessed for COF or other porous materials.[5a,b] Therefore, the characteristic XRD diffraction peak corresponding to the \(1.8\;\mathrm{nm}\) pore of COF \((2\theta=4.7^{\circ})^{[5c-f]}\) became gradually weakened and finally disappeared during the growth process with prolonged reaction times of \(1{-}30\,\mathrm{\min}\) (Figures S2a and S3, Supporting Information). There are also detected changes of two peaks shift to small angle (two peaks corresponding to (004) and (111) planes) for Mn-MOF, which is also ascribed to the molecular hybrid interaction of COF and Mn-MOF in the composite (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The FTIR peaks (Figure S2b, Supporting Information) of COF \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) stretching peak at \(1618\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ) and Mn-MOF \(\scriptstyle\left(-\mathrm{COO}^{-}\right.\) stretching at 1629, 1575, 1440, and \(1374~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ) can be all detected in the COF/Mn-MOF composite. It is worth mentioning that the redshift of \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}\) peak (located in the region of \(-\mathrm{COO^{-}}\) peak at \(1575~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ) in the composite should be assigned to the coordination effects between the N atoms of COF and the Mn centers of Mn-MOF.[6] Raman spectra (Figure S2c, Supporting Information) can also indicate the existence of Mn-MOF and COF in the COF/Mn-MOF composite. Furthermore, the \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR spectra of COF/Mn-MOF are largely identical to pristine COF and pristine Mn-MOF (Figure S2d, Supporting Information). However, the chemical shift variation of two C atoms next to
N atom in COF structure and C atoms of Mn-MOF should be ascribed to the coordination interaction between N atoms of COF and the paramagnetic Mn centers from MOF.[5d,7]
Figure 1a,b shows scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of layered-stacking bulk COF \((\approx\!80{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}\) in size) and Mn-MOF microsphere \((\approx\!9.9\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) in diameter). However, the COF/Mn-MOF hybrid is flower-like microsphere with rodlike petals and average diameter of \(\approx\!1.5\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Figure 1c,d). It is worth mentioning that the uniform flower-like microsphere morphology cannot be detected when the molar ratio of COF and MOF are changed. High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images in Figure 1e–g reveal several lattice fringes \((0.41\ \mathrm{nm}\) for the (004) plane of Mn-MOF and \(0.37\ \mathrm{nm}\) for the (001) plane of \(\mathrm{COF}^{[5c]}\) at the petal edge of the flower-like composite. The detected larger lattice fringe than pristine COF \((0.35\ \mathrm{nm})^{[5c]}\) could be ascribed to the expanded interlayered distance of COF along the \(c\) -direction because of the Mn-MOF interlinked hybridization into the adjacent layers of COF. Several elements of Mn, N, C, and O with uniform distribution can be confirmed by elemental mapping images in Figure 1h,i, further confirming the effective interlinked compositing mode of COF and Mn-MOF.
Different from previous COF–MOF hybrids,[3] where only core–shell or coating structure is achieved by simple combination between COF and MOF, this work presents molecular-level interlinked hybridization of two components in the COF/ Mn-MOF composite. As illustrated in Scheme 1, this is largely originated from the coordination interaction between N atoms
from the imine groups of COF and the Mn centers from the Mn-MOF as indicated by previous XRD, FTIR, and NMR results in Figures S2 and S3 (Supporting Information). To get more insight into the morphology transformation process, more efforts have been conducted to highlight the coordination interaction between COF and Mn-MOF. The spectra of N 1s and Mn 2p by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements (Figure S4, Supporting Information) clearly indicate the presence of \(\mathrm{Mn-N}\) interaction. Due to the strong coordination ability of Mn ions, especially its high coordination sites up to seven,[8] the six-connected Mn centers (coordinated by six O atoms from BTC ligands, Figure S1b, Supporting Information) in the Mn-MOF structure are probably expanded to be seven-coordination with another more connection with electron-rich N atoms from COF structure. Although the \(\mathrm{M}-\mathrm{N}\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Fe}, \mathrm{Ni}, \mathrm{Co}, \mathrm{etc.})\) coordination bond is very common in metal–organic frameworks with N-contained organic ligands, the effectively interlinked hybrid structure cannot be obtained from the combination of Fe or Co centered MOF with the COF (Figure S5, Supporting Information), which should be ascribed to their saturated six-coordination and short ionic radius.[2b,9]
The quantum mechanical calculation on COF/Mn-MOF hybrid structure was also performed using the CASTEP module of Materials Studio (MS) software. A molecular model of COF was established and the Mn-MOF structure was then introduced to construct the COF/Mn-MOF model. The distance between
Mn center from Mn-MOF and its neighboring N atom from COF was determined to be \(\approx 0.227\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Scheme 1c), which is in accordance with the \(\mathrm{Mn}-\mathrm{N}\) coordination bonding values \((0.199{-}0.236\ \mathrm{nm})\).[10] The optimized COF/Mn-MOF hybrid structure based on the \(\mathrm{Mn}-\mathrm{N}\) interaction has its own intrinsic energy \((37.57\ \mathrm{keV})\), which is substantially lower than the nonbonding structure. It also confirms that the \(\mathrm{Mn}-\mathrm{N}\) coordination promotes the effective compositing of COF and Mn-MOF. Furthermore, the binding energy of COF/Mn-MOF hybrid structure was calculated, and the largest binding energy \((6.449\ \mathrm{eV})\) indicates the most realizable and effective combination between COF and Mn-MOF along \( c \)-direction (Figure S6, Supporting Information).[11] In addition, the growth mechanism of flower-like microsphere was also explored based on the CASTEP quantum mechanical calculation (Scheme 1a; Figure S7, Supporting Information). During the synthetic process of pristine Mn-MOF with spherical morphology, similar growth rate along various directions can be achieved for the crystal nucleation, which agrees well with the calculation results of similar surface energies for three planes (Figure S7b, Supporting Information). For the COF/Mn-MOF, the 2D layered COF structure tends to disperse and stack on the surface of spherical structure (perpendicular to the radial growth direction) during the growth of Mn-MOF structure with the intercalated combination between them based on the \(\mathrm{Mn}-\mathrm{N}\) coordination interaction (Scheme 1a). The surface energies for different crystal growth directions were calculated to be 1.305,
2.440, and \(4.173\,\mathrm{~J~m}^{-2}\) along the \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\)-directions (corresponding to the (100), (010), and (001) lattice planes, respectively) (Figure S7a,c–e, Supporting Information). It is believed that the growth direction with larger surface energy exhibits faster crystal growth rate along this direction.[12] Therefore, the enhanced crystal growth rate can be detected for the COF/Mn-MOF along the \(c\)-direction, which is perpendicular to the 2D layered structure of COF. Based on the fast growth tendency of (001) lattice plane of Mn-MOF toward and through porous 2D layered COF structure, the flower-like microsphere of the COF/Mn-MOF composite consisting of interlinked COF and MOF structure (Scheme 1a,d) can be achieved. Furthermore, the BFDH method in the morphology module was also selected to give more illumination to the morphology evolution of COF/Mn-MOF hybrid structure. As shown in the theoretical morphology of one growth unit for COF/Mn-MOF structure (Figure S8, Supporting Information), the smallest \(d_{\mathrm{hkl}}\) value and lowest total facet area percentage \((\approx\!10.77\,\%)\) for the (001) face indicate the largest growth rate along the \(c\)-direction.[13] The calculated results can also be confirmed with the experimental morphology evolution with the increased reaction time. As indicated in Scheme 1a and Figure S9 (Supporting Information), the flower-like microsphere with increased sizes can be observed with the prolonged reaction times from 5 to \(30\;\mathrm{min}\). In addition, it also agrees well with the detected lattice planes for both COF and MOF in HRTEM images (Figure 1f,g), in which the substantially parallel (001) lattice planes of COF \((0.37\;\mathrm{nm})\) and (004) lattice planes of Mn-MOF \((0.41\,\mathrm{nm})\) can be detected along the \(c\)-direction, which agrees well with the growth direction for the rod-like petal. On the other hand, the interlinked Mn-MOF structure in COF/Mn-MOF also affects the 2D layered structure of COF, which leads to an enlarged interlamellar spacing from \(0.35\;\mathrm{nm}\) for pristine COF to \(\approx\!0.37\;\mathrm{nm}\) for the COF in the composite (Scheme 1d), as verified by HRTEM images in Figure 1f,g. The interlinked structure for the COF/Mn-MOF composite results in the reassembled microstructure of COF and MOF. The composite delivers a slightly larger surface area \((53.58\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) compared with pristine COF \((44.36\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and Mn-MOF \((15.73\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) along with hierarchical porous feature (Figure S10, Supporting Information), which is different with previous \(\mathrm{MOF}@C O F^{[3a]}\) or MOF-coated \(\mathrm{COF}^{[3c]}\) hybrids. The slightly enlarged surface area should be mainly ascribed to the more dispersed 2D COF in the COF/MOF composite, which leads to more exposed surface area compared to bulk COF with tightly stacked structure. Different synthetic procedures were also explored to synthesize COF/Mn-MOF. The direct one-pot synthesis of COF and Mn-MOF and the introduction of the as-prepared Mn-MOF into the synthesis process of COF did not produce interlinked composites (Figure S11, Supporting Information).
The sulfidation of COF/Mn-MOF through gas-phase (S powder as the S source) or liquid-phase (thioacetamide as the S source) resulted in the synthesis of MnS trapped in N/S codoped carbon with hollow microsphere \((\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-g}})\) or core–shell microsphere \((\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-l}})\), respectively (Figure 2a). A temperature of \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was selected to perform the sulfidation based on the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA)results (Figure S12, Supporting Information). XRD patterns (Figure S13a, Supporting Information) reveal the existence of cubic \(\alpha\)-MnS (JCPDS No. 06-0518) in two composites[14] with more disordered
carbon structure due to the N/S doping (Raman spectra in Figure S13b, Supporting Information).[15] XPS analyses (Figure S14, Supporting Information) along with the energy-dispersive X-ray spectra (EDS in Figure S15, Supporting Information) further confirm the N and S codoping, which may increase the defect density and enhance the electrical conductivity. The doped N/S contents can be determined for \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-g}}\) (N: \(6.02\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) ; S: \(1.40\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) ) and \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-l}}\) (N: \(6.23\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) ; S: \(0.22\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) ) based on elemental analysis (Table S1, Supporting Information) and TGA (Figure S16, Supporting Information) results. The \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-g}}\) composite, derived via gas-phase sulfidation process, exhibited hollow microsphere \((\approx\!600\;\mathrm{nm}\) in diameter) morphology (Figure 2b; Figure S17a–c, Supporting Information), which is largely due to the surface sulfidation and the concentration-dominated diffusion effect. While the core–shell microsphere with similar diameter size was observed for the \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-l}}\) composite obtained by the liquid-phase sulfidation process (Figure 2f; Figure S17d–f, Supporting Information). The core–shell structure should be ascribed to the uniform infusion of sulfur precursor liquid into the composite, which results in the sulfidation occurred simultaneously on both shell and core sections and the subsequent contraction of the product core at high temperature. Clear lattice fringes with interplanar distances of 0.32, 0.26, and \(0.18\;\mathrm{nm}\), consistent with the (111), (200), and (220) planes of \(\mathrm{MnS},^{[14b,15b]}\) can also be observed in the HRTEM images of \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-g}}\) and \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-l}}\) (Figure 2c,d,g,h). Elemental mapping images (Figure 2e,i) reveal the uniform distributions of five elements (Mn, S, C, N, and O) in two composites. Moreover, a larger specific surface area was detected for the \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-l}}\) core–shell microsphere \((257.3\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) than the \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-g}}\) hollow microsphere \((52.0\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) (Figure S18, Supporting Information). More importantly, the in situ N/S codoped carbon in both \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-g}}\) and \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-l}}\) is derived from the coordination interaction between N-containing COF and non-N-containing ligands from Mn-MOF via a simple one-step sulfidation process.
Up to date, more MOF or COF and their related materials have been adopted as candidates for lithium-ion batteries, sodium-ion batteries, and other energy storage applications.[16] The COF/Mn-MOF and its derived \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-g}}\) and \(\mathrm{MnS}@{\mathrm{NS-C-l}}\) in this work have also been explored on the electrochemical properties as evaluated as the anode materials for lithium-ion batteries (Figure 3; Figures S19 and S20, Supporting Information). Compared with pristine Mn-MOF \((\approx\!258\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 650 cycles) and COF \((\approx\!126\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 650 cycles), the COF/Mn-MOF composite undergoes a gradual activation process and exhibits a larger capacity of \(1015\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 650 cycles (Figure 3a). The long activation phenomenon can be largely ascribed to the gradual activation of deeply buried Mn centers and aromatic rings for lithium storage along with the expanded interlayered spacings for COF in the hybrid during repetitive lithium insertion/extraction. Similar activation phenomenon has been explored with more details in our previous COF@CNT electrode.[5c] The significantly increased reversible capacities for the COF/Mn-MOF in this work should be attributed to the contribution from two components of COF and Mn-MOF. The coordination interaction between COF and Mn-MOF endows each other with
morphology adjustment and lithium-storage functionality enrichment. Furthermore, the unique flower-like microsphere morphology for the COF/Mn-MOF hybrid with hierarchical porous structure is also favorable for the enhanced Li-storage properties of the COF/Mn-MOF electrode. XPS measurements on various electrodes (Figure 3b,c; Figure S21, Supporting Information) reveal that there is a reversible transformation between \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) and Mn metal in the lithiation and delithiation process (Figure 3b). It is in accordance with the CV results (Figure S19a, Supporting Information), in which the peak of \({\approx}0.72\mathrm{~V~}\) in the cathode scan and the peak of \({\approx}1.2\mathrm{~V~}\) at the anodic scan correspond to the reduction of \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) and then the oxidation of metallic Mn. However, these changes cannot be detected for pristine Mn-MOF (Figure 3c), indicating that \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) of pristine Mn-MOF is not active, which is in accordance with previous report.[4a] On the other hand, the molecular-level
interlinked combination with Mn-MOF also activate the aromatic benzene rings of COF in the COF/Mn-MOF composite for the lithium storage (Figure S21a–c, Supporting Information), which were not observed for pristine COF (Figure S21d–f, Supporting Information). The coordination-based COF/ Mn-MOF with interlinked structure can probably endow MOF components with loosened inner interaction between units and the COF components with interlamellar spacing expansion, which can facilitate the kinetics for deeply-buried Mn centers and \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}\) from aromatic rings. As indicated in Figure S22 (Supporting Information), the flower-like microsphere morphology with the maintained structure can be detected for the COF/ Mn-MOF hybrid after cycling. Furthermore, good lithiumstorage properties can also be detected for the COF/Mn-MOF derived \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C-g}\) \(\mathrm{[1068~\mAh~\g^{-1}}\) after 200 cycles) and \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C-l}\) (1017 mAh \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) after 200 cycles) (Figure 3d;
Figure S23, Supporting Information). TEM images (Figure S24, Supporting Information) reveal intact structures for two composites after repetitive cycling. Compared with previous MnS-related electrodes (Table S2, Supporting Information) and the benchmarked products of MOF-derived MnS@S-C composites (without COF-derived N doped carbon) of this work, the MnS@ NS-C-g and MnS@NS-C-l composites deliver larger reversible capacities and good cycling stability. It should be mainly ascribed to the existence of the in situ introduced N and S codoped porous carbon matrix, which would allow facilitated lithium ion transportation, enhanced lithium-reaction kinetic as well as more active sites for Li storage. The change from the diffusion-dominated (Figures S25a,b and S26a,b, Supporting Information) to the capacitance-controlled (Figures S25c,d and S26c,d, Supporting Information) redox reactions after
cycling can be detected for the MnS@NS-C electrodes. In addition, lithium-ion diffusion coefficients can be determined for the MnS@NS-C-g and MnS@NS-C-l electrodes based on the galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT, Figure S27, Supporting Information) measurements, which are both higher than the MnS@S-C-g and MnS@S-C-l products. Furthermore, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) results of the MnS@NS-C-g and MnS@NS-C-l after 1st and 200 cycles (Figure S28, Supporting Information) also demonstrated their activation process during cycling and the resultant facilitated charge-transfer properties. Besides, the slightly superior electrochemical properties for the MnS@NS-C-g compared with MnS@NS-C-l should be ascribed to the hollow microsphere morphology and higher capacitive contribution of the capacitance-dominated lithium-storage behavior for the former.
In summary, a coordination-induced molecularly interlinked COF/Mn-MOF composite is designed in this work. The \(\mathrm{Mn-N}\) coordination bond plays an important role in the formation of the interlinked composite structure, which can be determined well based on experimental characteristics and theoretical calculations. The adjustable morphology and microstructure for the COF/Mn-MOF endows it and its derived \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C}\) composites with enhanced electrochemical performances. Adopted as the anode material for lithium-ion batteries, the COF/Mn-MOF exhibits an extremely high reversible capacity of \(1015\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 650 cycles, which is ascribed to the involvement of metal centers \((\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\), conversion mechanism) and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) groups of benzene rings for lithium storage. Furthermore, the derived hollow or core–shell \(\mathrm{MnS@NS-C}\) composites via different sulfidation process both exhibit superior lithium-storage properties (above \(1000\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 200 cycles) compared with previous MnS-related electrodes. This work can shed light on the designable hybridization on porous organic frameworks to acquire more promising hybridization structures with molecular-level structural adjustment, nano/microsized morphology design, and properties optimization.
